// CRITICAL — behavior preservation depends on the helper NOT breaking on a match. resolveCheckpoint's original
// walk `break`s on the target match. The helper must NOT break on match — it collects EVERY matched id and keeps
// walking (so a compaction AFTER the target still yields mapped=[targetIdx]). resolveCheckpoint then takes
// mapped[mapped.length-1] = targetIdx = the original iTarget. (Hand-traced in research note §2.)

// CRITICAL — guard ORDER must mirror resolveCheckpoint step 4 exactly:
//   (1) yield = entryMessageYield(e)
//   (2) if (y < 0) break;                 // indeterminate FIRST
//   (3) if (msgCursor + y > messages.length) break;   // alignment-lost SECOND
//   (4) if matched → collect range          // match THIRD
//   (5) msgCursor += y                       // advance LAST
// Swapping order changes which entries get collected when a compaction/alignment-loss coincides with a match.

// CRITICAL — the helper is a NEW function, NOT resolveCheckpoint-with-a-flag. The two have DIFFERENT semantics:
// resolveCheckpoint REFUSES (returns null) on any indeterminacy in the walked range; the helper returns PARTIAL
// results (skip the indeterminate entry, keep what was deterministically mapped before it). resolveCheckpoint
// recovers its refuse-semantic by calling the helper with [targetId] and treating mapped.length===0 as 'refuse'.

// CRITICAL — entryMessageYield returns 1 for message/custom_message/branch_summary and -1 for compaction/unknown.
// So every mappable entry yields exactly 1 today; the 'range [msgCursor..msgCursor+yield-1]' is a single index.
// Implement the range as a small inner loop anyway (robust to a future yield>1; matches the contract's 'range' wording).

